  "**Profile & Learning Style:** Gal (age 6) communicates via AAC and strongly prefers interactive, hands-on apps (drawing, music) over passive activities. Core interests: facts, drawing, music, science (hands-on exploration: experiments, how things work, chemistry, weather), farm games, space topics (stars, moon, planets, rockets, astronauts), and programming (Code blocks, Games, Robots). Typically looks down and to the side during communication—this is normal.\n\n**Communication Patterns:** Uses 'I'm thinking about' button to initiate guessing mode. Prefers shorter, focused interactions with clear exit strategies. Frequently expresses tiredness via guessing mode. Responds well to direct activity suggestions (e.g., 'Want to draw a rocket?') rather than abstract menu browsing. When browsing multiple options without committing, offer specific, interest-based prompts instead of waiting for selection.\n\n**Safety & Notable Events:** (1) Expressed fear multiple times and seeks home/safety when scared (trigger unidentified—remain alert and reassuring). (2) Lost hat at school (confirmed April 9); shows persistence in communicating about it. (3) April 13: Expressed frustration and pressed 'Scissors' three times in succession—may indicate scissors-related incident at school or home; monitor for clarification and continue offering reassurance and specific activities when frustrated.\n\n**Session Progress:** Gal shows sustained interest in space exploration and rockets as a strong motivator for engagement, even when expressing tiredness. Responds well to direct activity suggestions and navigates conversation flow smoothly when space/rocket topics are introduced. Rockets are an effective engagement tool."
]

